 GAIL’s share were seen trading green on Tuesday after the company reported strong second quarter results. Net profit rose 110% to Rs 4,650 crore on growth in gas marketing business The company's stocks were trading at Rs 181.27 on the NSE, up Rs 4.27 or 2.41% at 01:15 pm. Nomura maintains ‘Buy’ Nomura has maintained its 'Buy' rating on GAIL and raised its target price to Rs 210 from Rs 195, following a broad-based beat in the company's second-quarter results and an increase in management's profit guidance for gas marketing. GAIL 's adjusted EBITDA for the September quarter stood at Rs 64.1 billion, up 1 percent quarter-on-quarter and 101 percent year-on-year. The figure was 22 percent above Nomura's estimate, with all business segments except LPG/LHC reporting better-than-expected performance, led by gas marketing. Management doubled its FY27 gas marketing EBIT guidance to Rs 90-95 billion from Rs 45 billion earlier. It also raised its gas transmission volume guidance to 126-127 million standard cubic metres per day (mmscmd) from 123 mmscmd. Nomura said there could be further upside to transmission volumes if the West Asia crisis is resolved earlier than expected or spot LNG prices correct from current record levels due to an El Niño-driven impact on winter severity. In gas transmission, volumes reached 130.1 mmscmd in the second quarter, up 6 percent sequentially and 4 percent above Nomura's estimate. The brokerage has raised its volume estimates by around 3 percent for FY27 and FY28. Gas marketing volumes rose 3 percent sequentially to 97.2 mmscmd, 1 percent ahead of estimates. Segment EBIT was Rs 32.8 billion, 23 percent above Nomura's estimate of Rs 26.7 billion, supported by a sharp increase in the spread between Henry Hub-linked LNG and spot LNG. While management expects FY27 EBIT at Rs 90-95 billion, Nomura expects it to return to a normal run rate of Rs 45-50 billion in FY28. LPG/LHC segment EBIT fell 43 percent sequentially to Rs 4.4 billion, despite a 16 percent increase in sales volumes, due to lower LPG realisations. Petrochemical sales volumes rose nearly five-fold sequentially to 188,000 tonnes on better feedstock availability. Segment EBIT improved to a loss of Rs 0.2 billion as realisations increased. Management expects profitability to remain at current levels. Nomura has raised its FY27 consolidated EBITDA estimate by 29%, largely on account of a 74% increase in its gas marketing EBITDA estimate. The revised target price of Rs 210 is based on a sum-of-the-parts valuation. GAIL is trading at 7.1 times its FY28 estimated EV/EBITDA and 1.1 times its FY28 estimated price-to-book value, broadly in line with historical averages. Nomura also noted that the company has announced an IPO of its unlisted subsidiary GAIL Gas, though it does not expect meaningful value unlocking from the listing in the immediate future. 
